|~ Cross-family invariant tag-bindings. Each error tag below is
   stamped on the error value's JS-header `tag` slot by a runtime
   site that no single operand family owns — the parser, the AST
   codec, the evaluator
   dispatcher, the primitive registry, the session lifecycle, the
   projection segment-walker, the combinator track-dispatcher,
   the render-path round-trip guards. Per-operand errors live
   inline in the operand's own family file
   (lib/qlang/operand/<family>.qlang); the errors of the kinds'
   constructors (::verb / ::quote / ::builtin) live next to their
   constructors in lib/qlang/tag.qlang.

   Section layout, top to bottom:
     0. ::builtin — the primordial tag
     1. Projection segment-miss family
     2. Distribute / merge combinator subject mismatches
     3. Tag-binding constructor invariants
     4. Effect-laundering at parse and call seams
     5. Identifier-lookup and parse-time failures
     6. AST codec invariants
     7. JSON codec invariants — plain and tagged
     8. Dispatch wrapper invariants
     9. Evaluator dispatch invariants
    10. Primitive registry invariants
    11. Session lifecycle invariants
    12. Render-path invariants
    13. Error-value mint invariants
    14. Evaluation depth budget
    15. Catalog source loading ~|

|~ ── 0. ::builtin — the primordial tag ─────────────────── ~|

::builtin
  |~~ Tag-binding declaration shape. Every catalog entry — operand
      declarations, the declarations of kinds, error
      tag-binding declarations — uses ::builtin{…} as its body.

        :name ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/<name> …catalog metadata}
          binds a runtime callback under the impl handle.

        ::Name ::builtin{…tag-side metadata}
          declares an identity (an error tag, a kind) carrying
          docs / category / position / expectedType metadata that
          downstream `::Name | source / docs / examples` exposes.

        ::Name (doc-prefix-only, no body)
          equivalent to `::Name ::builtin{}` — declares an identity
          whose only payload is the attached docs.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/builtin}

::BuiltinPayloadNotMapError
  |~~ `::builtin<payload>` payload must be a Map descriptor.
      User-site invocations on a String / Vec / Scalar payload
      fire this tag rather than silently destructure-iterating
      the payload into a garbage descriptor.

      ~(
        ::builtin"hello"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::BuiltinPayloadNotMapError ::string]
       )
      ~(
        ::builtin[1 2 3]
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::BuiltinPayloadNotMapError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|

::BuiltinImplNotPrimitiveKeyError
  |~~ A descriptor a query assembled from data dispatches through
      its `:impl` handle keyword — `::builtin{:impl
      :qlang/prim/type}` binds and fires like a catalog entry.
      Catalog descriptors carry the resolved callable on the
      `BUILTIN_IMPL_SLOT` JS-header slot instead, so the handle
      reading below fires only for the data-assembled shape, and
      only when `:impl` holds something the primitive registry
      cannot be keyed by.

      ~(
        ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/type} | :k / | {:a 1} | k
        |  eq ::map
       )
      ~(
        ::builtin{:impl 42} | :bogus / | 1 | bogus
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::BuiltinImplNotPrimitiveKeyError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::BuiltinImplOfVerbError
  |~~ The primitive of a built-in declared as a verb on its noun is
      called through the head of its declaration, which checks what it
      takes [D72]; a descriptor a query assembled from data that names
      it is refused, and the verb is called by its name or its address.

      ~(
        ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/count} | :c / | [1 2 3] | c
        !| [type /impl]
        |  eq [::BuiltinImplOfVerbError :qlang/prim/count]
       )
  ~~|

::ForeignFailureError
  |~~ A failure of the host's implementation: an error of the language
      the runtime is written in escaped an operand, which is a defect
      of that operand's host. `:name` carries the host's class name,
      `:message` its text, `:operand` the step that ran it and
      `:causes` the chain of host errors behind it, each with its own
      `:name` and `:message`. The step and the input are those of
      any error; recovery is a `!|` step, and the repair belongs to
      the host.
  ~~|

|~ ── 1. Projection segment-miss family ──────────────────── ~|

::ProjectionFieldNotOnValueClassError
  |~~ Projection segment is not a publicly-projectable field
      on the subject, a doc.

      ~(
        |~~ prose ~~| | /missing
        !| [type /key]
        |  eq [::ProjectionFieldNotOnValueClassError "missing"]
       )
  ~~|

::ProjectionIndexOutOfBoundsError
  |~~ Projection `/n` — the integer index resolves outside
      the Vec or Set subject's `[0, length)` range.

      ~(
        [10 20 30] | /99
        !| [type /index /length]
        |  eq [::ProjectionIndexOutOfBoundsError 99 3]
       )
  ~~|

::ProjectionKeyNotInMapError
  |~~ Projection `/key` — the named key is absent from the
      Map subject (strict-miss). Use `at :key` for
      null-coalescing access.

      ~(
        {:a 1} | /b
        !| [type /key]
        |  eq [::ProjectionKeyNotInMapError "b"]
       )
  ~~|

::ProjectionSubjectNotProjectableError
  |~~ Projection `/key` requires a map, a vector, a set or a tagged
      value as its subject; null and other shapes lift to this error.

      ~(
        42 | /foo
        !| [type /key /actualType]
        |  eq [::ProjectionSubjectNotProjectableError "foo" ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::ProjectionSequenceKeyNotIntegerError
  |~~ Projection segment is a non-numeric string but the
      subject is a Vec or Set — sequence indices must be integer
      offsets (insertion-order on a Set per §Set in
      qlang-spec.md).

      ~(
        [10 20 30] | /name
        !| [type /key]
        |  eq [::ProjectionSequenceKeyNotIntegerError "name"]
       )
       ~(
        #[:a :b :c] | /name
        !| [type /key]
        |  eq [::ProjectionSequenceKeyNotIntegerError "name"]
       )
  ~~|

|~ ── 2. Distribute combinator subject mismatch ─────────── ~|

::DistributeSubjectNotSequenceError
  |~~ Distribute combinator (`*`) — subject must be a Vec or Set
      for per-element forking; a Set distributes into a Vec result.

      ~(
        42 * add 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::DistributeSubjectNotSequenceError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

|~ ── 3. Tag-binding constructor invariants ─────────────── ~|

::TagBindingHasNoConstructorError
  |~~ Tag-binding has no registered constructor — `:impl`
      carries an unexpected value (neither a `:qlang/prim/*`
      keyword nor a Quote-impl body). Descriptor fields:
      `:payloadValue` / `:payloadType`, `:actualValue` /
      `:actualType`, `:expectedType [:keyword :quote]`.
  ~~|

::TaggedLitNotTagBindingError
  |~~ `::tag` constructor invocation — the env binding under
      `::tag` is not a tag-binding descriptor Map.

      ~(
        ::badType 42 | ::badType[]
        !| [type /tag /actualType]
        |  eq [::TaggedLitNotTagBindingError :badType ::number]
       )
  ~~|

|~ ── 4. Effect-laundering at parse and call seams ──────── ~|

::EffectLaunderingAtBindStepParseError
  |~~ BindStep declaration body references an `@`-effectful
      identifier, but the binding name is not `@`-prefixed —
      laundering caught at parse time.

      ~(
        1 | (:bad ::verb~(@callers) | bad)
        !| [type /bindingName /effectfulName]
        |  eq [::EffectLaunderingAtBindStepParseError
                :bad :@callers]
       )
  ~~|

|~ ── 5. Identifier-lookup and parse-time failures ───────── ~|

::BindNameDeclaredTwiceError
  |~~ A name is declared once in a scope: the steps of a pipeline
      that the pipe or the fail track joins share one env, and a
      declaration of a name one of them declared before is refused
      where it stands. A group, an element, a modifier, a quote and
      a step joined by `*` open scopes of their own, where a name
      of the scope around them is shadowed.

      ~(
        :k 1 | :k 2
        !| [type /name]
        |  eq [::BindNameDeclaredTwiceError :k]
       )
      ~(:k 1 | (:k 2 | k) | eq 2)
  ~~|

::ApplyToNonFunctionError
  |~~ A name the scope binds to a value takes no modifiers.
      `:addresses` names where a verb of that name lives, one the
      declaration shadows among them, which a call by address reaches.

      ~(
        5 | :five / | five 42
        !| [type /name /actualType]
        |  eq [::ApplyToNonFunctionError :five ::number]
       )
      ~(:sort 5 | [3 1] | sort ~(/a) !| /addresses | has ::vec/sort)
  ~~|

::EffectLaunderingAtCallError
  |~~ Identifier resolves to an `@`-effectful function, but the
      lookup name is not `@`-prefixed — laundering caught at the
      call site. Host-boundary error: fires through env-installed
      bindings (`use`, `session.bind`) that bypass the parse-time
      AST scan.
  ~~|

::UnresolvedIdentifierError
  |~~ Identifier lookup failed — no env binding under that name.
      `:identifierName` holds the name as typed, and `:nearest` the
      names of the value namespace it was likely meant to be: those
      within an edit distance of a third of its length, one at least,
      and of those the nearest alone, in the order of their spelling.
      An edit is an insertion, a deletion, a substitution or a swap of
      two neighbours. A name with nothing near holds `[]`.

      ~(
        thisIsNotDefined
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::UnresolvedIdentifierError]
       )
      ~(fist !| /nearest | eq [:first])
      ~(:limit 3 | limti !| /nearest | eq [:limit])
      ~(zzzzqqq !| /nearest | eq [])
  ~~|

::UnresolvedAddressError
  |~~ A name with a path names no verb: no verb of its last name
      lives on the noun of its path. `:address` holds the address as
      a tag name, written short, which the axes read.

      ~(
        [1 2 3] | vec/nothing
        !| [type /address]
        |  eq [::UnresolvedAddressError ::vec/nothing]
       )
  ~~|

::ParseError
  |~~ Parser failure — source did not match the grammar.

      ~(
        "42 |" | parse
        !| type | spec | /category
        |  eq :parseError
       )
  ~~|


|~ ── 6. JSON codec invariants — plain and tagged ──────── ~|

::MalformedTaggedJSONError
  |~~ `fromTaggedJSON` received a JSON shape it does not
      recognise as a tagged-value envelope.
  ~~|

::FromPlainNumberNotFiniteError
  |~~ JSON carries no NaN and no infinity, yet `JSON.parse` reads a
      magnitude past the double range as one (`1e400` lifts to
      Infinity). The plain-JSON lift refuses it, so a piped document
      cannot seed a pipeline with a value the language does not
      admit. Reached through `parseJson` or a host's lift of its
      input, the command line's stdin among them, never from a qlang
      literal, which the parser refuses at its own site.

      ~("1e400" | parseJson !| type | eq ::FromPlainNumberNotFiniteError)
  ~~|

::TaggedJSONNumberNotFiniteError
  |~~ Same reading on the tagged-JSON wire: a decoded number past
      the finite double range refuses rather than restoring a
      session or a fixture around a value no literal renders back.
  ~~|

::TaggedJSONUnencodableValueError
  |~~ `toTaggedJSON` cannot encode a function value, nor the record
      of a binding that holds one, since a function a host bound has no
      reading as data.
  ~~|

|~ ── 7. Dispatch wrapper invariants ───────────────────── ~|

::HigherOrderOpArityMismatchError
  |~~ Dispatch invariant — `higherOrderOp` impl received an
      unexpected captured-arg count.
  ~~|

::HigherOrderOpVariadicMissingCapturedError
  |~~ Dispatch invariant — `higherOrderOpVariadic` registration
      is missing the `captured` range argument.
  ~~|

::NullaryOpArgsProvidedError
  |~~ Nullary operand received captured arguments.

      ~(
        {:a 1} | type 42
        !| [type /operandName]
        |  eq [::NullaryOpArgsProvidedError :type]
       )
  ~~|

::StateOpArityMismatchError
  |~~ `stateOp` impl received an unexpected captured-arg count.

      ~(
        examples :filter
        !| [type /operandName]
        |  eq [::StateOpArityMismatchError :examples]
       )
  ~~|

::StateOpVariadicMissingCapturedError
  |~~ Dispatch invariant — `stateOpVariadic` registration is
      missing the `captured` range argument.
  ~~|

::ValueOpArityMismatchError
  |~~ Dispatch invariant — `valueOp` impl received an unexpected
      captured-arg count for its declared arity.
  ~~|

|~ ── 8. Evaluator dispatch invariants ─────────────────── ~|

::UnknownAstNodeTypeError
  |~~ Evaluator dispatch — encountered an AST node whose `.type`
      is not in `AST_NODE_EVALUATORS`. Runtime invariant
      violation.
  ~~|

::UnknownCombinatorKindError
  |~~ Combinator dispatch — encountered a combinator kind not
      in `COMBINATOR_EVALUATORS`. Runtime invariant violation.
  ~~|

::Rule10ArityOverflowError
  |~~ Operand call supplied more captured arguments than the
      operand's declared maximum arity.

      ~(
        {:a 1} | type 1 2
        !| [type /operandName]
        |  eq [::Rule10ArityOverflowError :type]
       )
  ~~|

|~ ── 9. Primitive registry invariants ─────────────────── ~|

::PrimitiveKeyAlreadyBoundError
  |~~ Primitive registry — two runtime modules tried to bind
      the same primitive name.
  ~~|

::ThrowSiteSpecAlreadyRecordedError
  |~~ Throw-site spec registry — two per-site error classes were
      declared under one name. The name is the identity this
      catalog's `::Tag` binding and the stamped `spec` both read,
      so the second declaration would speak for both sites.
  ~~|

::PrimitiveKeyNotStringError
  |~~ Primitive registry — bind() received a non-string
      handle.
  ~~|

::PrimitiveKeyUnboundError
  |~~ Primitive registry — `:impl` handle on a descriptor
      Map points to a primitive that was never bound.
  ~~|

::PrimitiveRegistrySealedError
  |~~ Primitive registry — bind() invoked against a sealed
      registry. langRuntime seals at bootstrap completion;
      later bind sites must split into a fresh
      createPrimitiveRegistry() instance.
  ~~|

|~ ── 10. Session lifecycle invariants ──────────────────── ~|

::SessionPayloadInvalidError
  |~~ Session deserialization — payload is malformed (missing
      required fields).
  ~~|

::SessionSchemaVersionMismatchError
  |~~ Session deserialization — payload `:schemaVersion` does
      not match SESSION_SCHEMA_VERSION.
  ~~|

|~ ── 11. Render-path invariants ───────────────────────── ~|

::FunctionValueLeakedToPrintError
  |~~ `printValue` / `toPlain` invariant — a raw function
      value reached render. Install a host operand through a
      locator returning `{ source, impls }` so the namespace pass
      stamps the callable onto the catalog descriptor's
      `BUILTIN_IMPL_SLOT` JS-header slot
      (`cli/src/cli-locator.mjs` is the reference shape); a
      callable handed straight to `session.bind` carries no qlang
      literal, which is what this tag reports.
  ~~|

::NumberNotFiniteLeakedToPrintError
  |~~ A qlang Number is a finite double. Source cannot mint an
      infinity or a NaN — the parser refuses the literal and every
      arithmetic site lifts a numericDomain error — so this tag
      reports a value a host installed through `session.bind` or a
      locator's `impls` map. Render and the JSON boundary are where
      such a value becomes observable: `printValue` would answer
      `Infinity`, which no production reads back, and `json` would
      answer `null`. `:actualValue` carries the offending magnitude
      as a String, the one shape that renders.
  ~~|

::ToPlainUnencodableValueError
  |~~ `toPlain` invariant — the lossy plain-JSON codec met a shape
      with no JSON form, a host value an embedder installed. Route the
      value through `tjson` / `toTaggedJSON` when the round-trip has
      to stay lossless, or project around the slot.
  ~~|

|~ ── 12. Error-value mint invariants ──────────────────── ~|

::ErrorTrailNotQuoteError
  |~~ `:trail` on an error descriptor is runtime-owned: a
      Quote-value carrying the joined pipeline-suffix source, or
      `null` before any deflection. A literal or a re-lift that
      stamps any other value under `:trail` fires this error at
      mint time, so the fail-track never carries a suffix that
      `apply` cannot replay. To drop an accumulated suffix before
      re-lift, stamp `:trail null` inside the fail-apply step.

      ~(
        !{:kind :oops :trail [1 2]}
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ErrorTrailNotQuoteError ::vec]
       )
      ~(
        !{:kind :oops} | count
        !| union {:trail []} | error
        !| type | eq ::ErrorTrailNotQuoteError
       )
      ~(
        !{:kind :oops} | count
        !| union {:trail null} | error | add 1
        !| /trail | eq ~(| add 1)
       )
  ~~|

|~ ── 13. Evaluation depth budget ───────────────────────── ~|

::EvaluationDepthExceededError
  |~~ The evaluator descends one frame per verb body,
      captured-arg lambda, `apply` re-entry, Quote-bodied
      tag constructor, doc-segment literal, and locator-loaded
      module; the frame past `EVAL_DEPTH_LIMIT` (state.mjs) lifts
      this error with `:depth` (the refused frame) and `:limit`
      (the budget) on the descriptor. A verb that calls itself
      without a base case surfaces here on the fail-track while the
      host's heap stays bounded.

      ~(
        :inf ::verb~(add 1 | inf) | 0 | inf
        !| type | eq ::EvaluationDepthExceededError
       )
      ~(
        :q ~(apply q) | apply q
        !| gt /depth /limit
       )
  ~~|

|~ ── 14. Catalog source loading ────────────────────────── ~|

::SourceLoadError
  |~~ The platform resolved a `#qlang/<ns>` logical name to a path
      or URL the host could not read. The descriptor carries
      `:host` (`:node` / `:web`), `:logicalName`, `:sourceLocation`,
      and either the underlying `:cause` or the HTTP `:status`. A
      host that ships its own catalog answers the `:qlang/locator`
      contract and never reaches the platform loader.
  ~~|

::BootstrapCatalogNotLoadedError
  |~~ The catalog root is one `use […]` step, so a family source
      the locator resolves but the parser refuses leaves the env
      without that family while the failure rides out as the root's
      pipeValue. The bootstrap reads that pipeValue, so the failing
      family source is the diagnostic and `:tagName` names what the
      root answered with.
  ~~|

::BootstrapRootMissingError
  |~~ `#qlang/core` resolved to nothing, so the bootstrap has no
      root module to evaluate. The host adds the entry to
      `package.json#imports` (Node) or to the import map (browser),
      or hands `langRuntime({ locator })` a locator of its own.
  ~~|
